In the established cohort of adult patients with CKD who were taking an RAS inhibitors before hospital admission and subsequently developed AKI, compare patients according to whether the ACE inhibitor/ARB was continued or re-prescribed at hospital discharge versus not continued at discharge.

Use only structured Epic/EHR data. Do not use progress notes, discharge summaries, nephrology notes, radiology narrative, free-text documentation, or NLP-extracted variables.

First identify the available structured tables and fields for the following characteristics, then build the comparison dataset.

Demographics:
- Age at admission
- Sex
- Race
- Ethnicity
- BMI using structured height and weight

Baseline kidney characteristics:
- Baseline serum creatinine
- Baseline eGFR
- CKD stage derived from pre-admission kidney function
- Baseline potassium
- Baseline bicarbonate/CO2
- UACR
- UPCR
- Urine albumin
- Urine protein
- Serum albumin

Comorbidities using structured diagnosis/problem-list codes:
- Hypertension
- Diabetes mellitus
- Heart failure
- HFrEF if a structured diagnosis code is available
- Coronary artery disease
- Prior myocardial infarction
- Peripheral arterial disease
- Cerebrovascular disease/stroke
- Atrial fibrillation
- Chronic liver disease
- Malignancy
- Dementia

Pre-admission medications using structured medication records:
- ACE inhibitor
- ARB
- Specific ACE inhibitor/ARB
- Loop diuretic
- Thiazide/thiazide-like diuretic
- Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist
- SGLT2 inhibitor
- Beta-blocker
- Calcium-channel blocker
- Potassium supplement
- Potassium binder
- NSAID

AKI severity:
- Admission serum creatinine
- Peak serum creatinine during hospitalization
- Admission BUN
- Peak BUN
- AKI stage derived from structured creatinine data
- Lowest eGFR during hospitalization
- Dialysis during hospitalization using structured procedure/order data
- CRRT using structured procedure/order data
- Intermittent hemodialysis using structured procedure/order data

Electrolyte and acid-base characteristics:
- Admission potassium
- Maximum potassium during hospitalization
- Most recent potassium before discharge
- Admission bicarbonate/CO2
- Minimum bicarbonate/CO2 during hospitalization
- Most recent bicarbonate/CO2 before discharge
- Admission sodium
- Minimum sodium
- Maximum sodium

Hemodynamic characteristics using structured vital-sign data:
- Admission systolic blood pressure
- Admission diastolic blood pressure
- Lowest systolic blood pressure during hospitalization
- Lowest mean arterial pressure during hospitalization, if available
- Most recent systolic blood pressure before discharge
- Most recent diastolic blood pressure before discharge
- Mean systolic blood pressure during the final 24 hours before discharge
- Mean diastolic blood pressure during the final 24 hours before discharge

Illness severity using structured encounter, medication, and procedure data:
- ICU admission
- ICU length of stay
- Vasopressor administration
- Mechanical ventilation
- Hospital length of stay
- Transfer to ICU
- Number of ICU days

Structured acute diagnoses/events:
- Sepsis
- Septic shock
- Hypotension
- Acute heart failure
- Cardiogenic shock
- Volume depletion/dehydration
- Acute myocardial infarction
- Major surgery during admission
- Urinary obstruction
- Contrast exposure if available as a structured medication/procedure record

Kidney recovery near discharge:
- Most recent serum creatinine before discharge
- Most recent eGFR before discharge
- Serum creatinine 24 hours before discharge, if available
- Serum creatinine 48 hours before discharge, if available
- Serum creatinine 72 hours before discharge, if available
- Absolute difference between peak and discharge creatinine
- Difference between baseline and discharge creatinine
- Ratio of discharge creatinine to baseline creatinine, if supported by the analytic environment
- Persistent AKI at discharge using a prespecified structured creatinine-based definition
- Dialysis dependence at discharge using structured dialysis orders/procedures and discharge medication/order data where available

Discharge medications:
- ACE inhibitor prescribed at discharge
- ARB prescribed at discharge
- Loop diuretic
- Thiazide/thiazide-like diuretic
- Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist
- SGLT2 inhibitor
- Beta-blocker
- Calcium-channel blocker
- Potassium binder
- Potassium supplement
- Number of antihypertensive medication classes prescribed at discharge

Discharge status using structured encounter/ADT fields:
- Discharge disposition
- Home
- Home with services
- Skilled nursing facility
- Inpatient rehabilitation
- Hospice
- Other structured discharge location

For every variable:
1. Use structured Epic/EHR data only.
2. Identify the exact source table, field, code set, medication concept, procedure concept, flowsheet field, or laboratory concept used.
3. Do not infer a diagnosis or clinical condition from free text.
4. Do not search narrative notes.
5. Clearly define the time window for baseline, hospitalization, and discharge measurements.
6. For discharge laboratory and vital-sign variables, use the closest structured measurement before the discharge timestamp.
7. Report missingness for every variable.
8. Do not silently exclude patients with missing data.
9. If a requested variable is not reliably available as structured data, mark it as unavailable rather than approximating it from narrative documentation.

Create a comparison table with:
- Overall cohort
- RASi continued/re-prescribed at discharge
- RASi not continued at discharge
- Appropriate descriptive statistics for each variable
- Standardized mean difference for each characteristic, if supported

Also create a structured-data dictionary with:
variable name | Epic source/table | field/concept/code | time window | derivation logic | missingness

The purpose is to identify objective structured characteristics that may influence the clinician's decision to continue versus withhold ACE inhibitor/ARB therapy at hospital discharge after AKI.
